+## ISSUE-013 — Adding GA4 requires consent and compatible script policy
+
+- **Date:** 2026-10-10
+- **Status:** Resolved
+- **Area:** Audience measurement, visitor privacy and Content Security Policy.
+- **Symptoms:** No stock-website GA4 property or tag existed. The site's self-only script policy would block Google's tag; no analytics choice or privacy page was present.
+- **Cause:** The original static site had no third-party analytics integration.
+- **Solution:** A separate GA4 property/web stream and basic consent integration. Load Google's tag only after acceptance, keep advertising features denied, strip URL parameters, offer withdrawal and publish a factual privacy page. Add only the Google tag/analytics hosts to the existing CSP directives; do not enable arbitrary or inline script execution. Local previews cannot initialize tracking.
+- **Why this solution:** Supports the requested traffic measurement while preserving a visitor's choice and the existing static architecture. Advanced consent mode would send cookieless pings before acceptance, so basic mode was selected. Resulting counts omit non-consenting/blocked visits and should not be compared directly with Cloudflare requests.
+- **Verification:** Five lifecycle tests cover no pre-consent loading, rejection persistence, one-time loading, URL redaction, withdrawal/cookie removal, expiry, preview isolation and blocked local storage. The full website suite and production build pass. Live observations are in VERIFICATION.md.
+- **Follow-up:** Use the precise metric and period requested by an ad network. Analytics is not a retrospective traffic history or an approval guarantee.
+- **References:** `web/analytics.js`, `web/_headers`, `scripts/site.mjs`, `tests/analytics.test.js`; [Google basic consent mode](https://developers.google.com/tag-platform/security/concepts/consent-mode).
